Implemented in `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:15-27`.

**What changed:**
- Added `public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable { case malformedFormat }`.
- Added `public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note`, which splits `text` on the literal `"\n---\n"` separator and builds a `Note` — id, title, pinned (parts[2] == "1"), body, and tags via the existing `extractTags(from:)`.
- Throws `NoteParseError.malformedFormat` unless the text splits into exactly 4 parts (no guessing, no defaults, no crash).

**Verified:**
- `swift build` and `swift test` pass (tests run with `--disable-sandbox` since the sandbox is blocked in this environment).
- Runtime edge-case harness: valid pinned/unpinned parse correctly; too-few parts, too-many parts (extra separator), and no-separator all throw `malformedFormat`; empty body yields `tags=[]`.

**Note:** I dropped `maxSplits` from `String.split` so that an extra separator (which would otherwise be absorbed into the last field) is correctly rejected as malformed rather than silently accepted — matching the "exactly four parts" requirement. The fixture's Rust `store.rs` uses `splitn(4, SEP)` which keeps extra separators in the final field; I chose the stricter interpretation since the task explicitly says to throw rather than guess.


